 ## Overview
-Rendering Pipelines and Passes define the structural organization and execution flow required to transform volumetric data or radiance fields into a 2D image, typically within a GPU-accelerated context. This family encompasses both object-order splatting pipelines (rasterizing primitives) and image-order ray-marching pipelines (sampling volumes), solving the problem of managing transparency, occlusion, and attribute interpolation for large datasets. It sets itself apart from monolithic implementations by decoupling geometric traversal, shading, and compositing into distinct, reusable stages or passes, enabling modular integration of different radiance field representations and hardware acceleration techniques.
